{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1303594-exalted-adventure-paths","id":1303594,"title":"Exalted Adventure Paths","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-04-08T13:09:29-07:00","id":1303594,"title":"Exalted Adventure Paths","content":"Is there one or more exalted adventure paths? If not, has anyone in the community considered making one? I think it could really help people (me for example) get started :-)<br /> <br /> I have read some of the quests here on the forum, so I know there are some really good storytellers and I can read that many are also quite happy with the system. I would love to make a campaign by myself, but for one I am not very experienced as a GM in any system at not at all exalted, and it has been difficult to get so much spare time since I got a kid and a job.<br /> <br /> I have not tried writing an adventure path (obviously), but maybe just writing down the storyline and encounters from a group that plays frequently could do the trick? I realize that it is harder to write something like an adventure path for exalted than for e.g. pathfinder, but that just makes the task of starting as a GM more daunting.<br /> <br /> Edit: I have been away for some time, I saw that dragonblooded has been released, I guess it would be a lot easier to make something like an adventure path for them? It is easier for them to have a superior giving missions and keeping them on course (or at least at in the same sea).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Also, look at <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Ow3IPUb4QtgMb1y2evMSGlJxE2VmTEOvyhJDu2eMAAo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">the exalted fan collection</a> that we are making.","edited":{"userid":80,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/80-truemonk","user":"TrueMonk","date":". 04-08-2019, 04:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/80-truemonk","name":"TrueMonk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-08T14:21:39-07:00","id":1303617,"title":"","content":"From my experience as a player and (to a far lesser degree) an ST, setting out a predetermined campaign path doesn't work all that well with Exalted. Here's what I've cobbled together from what I've experienced as a player, what I've done in my minor ST experience, and what people have said:<br /> <br /> Start by working with your players to figure out what you want to play. I suggest you approach this in terms of where and how the game starts more than anything else. If there are specific things you think you'd like to have happen, put that forth to your players to see if they'd want that too. Ask them if they have specific desires---if someone wants to punch a Deathlord, it helps for you to know that from the outset. Likewise, if somebody wants sailing to happen, that should be known at the start so you don't get a scenario where the high-seas adventure has no characters with Sail, or where you have a Sail Supernal Eclipse who doesn't have anywhere to be an awesome captain (note that even if they could later go somewhere Sail-worthy, it'd still be pretty disappointing for them not to be able to use their Supernal at all for a while).<br /> <br /> Once you have that, make sure you have a solid general idea of where and how you're starting, and what the overall thrust of the game will be, and make sure the players all understand. &quot;This game will be a kingdom-building saga on the shores of the Dreaming Sea, starting in a small fishing village where the Circle has been drawn together by some strange fate&quot; is a pretty good synopsis. &quot;This game will start in Grieve and will pit the Circle against the armies of the fae and the dead in both martial and political arenas&quot; is another.<br /> <br /> When your players have their characters done, try to draw elements from their characters. Backstories can hold a wealth of potential allies, rivals, challenges, etc. A character defined by a hatred of slavery should probably be faced with situations where that hatred comes up.<br /> <br /> Rather than plotting out the game in a predetermined way, plot out the hypothetical sequence of events that would occur if the players did nothing. For example, &quot;Without player interference, the pirate queen Star of Avarice will sack the fishing village and try to sell the captives to the local raksha cataphract Saragos to acquire a magic talisman to enhance her ship. However, Saragos will instead manipulate her into being his pawn, and will use her forces to make a series of raids on various coastal cities with his hobgoblin army. This will draw attention from the Wyld Hunt.&quot; is a simplified but decent framework---you've got a solid idea and get a little less specific each level down from there, because you probably don't want to spend lots of time and energy specifically nailing down everything about the prospective timeline right at the start. After all, the whole point of this timeline is to be deviated from, since your players are hardly likely to sit around and do nothing.<br /> <br /> Then, for the various events you've mapped out, generally sketch out possible deviations. You don't need to cover everything or be super-specific, since you want to be flexible, but having these general ideas will help you be able to respond to events. For example, &quot;If the Circle chases off Star of Avarice, she will probably want to go and build her forces to seek revenge later. If they kill or otherwise neutralize her, this will draw the interest of her rival, the seafaring sorcerer Khandar. If they convince her to cooperate with them, she will have useful resources and info, but associating with her may bring trouble to the Circle.&quot; covers the basics.<br /> <br /> This approach is designed to allow the players to feel like they're driving the game forward, while also letting you steer the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8026-beans","name":"Beans","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8026_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"472"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-08T14:42:50-07:00","id":1303619,"title":"","content":"Exalted 2nd edition had some adventures. I have not read them, but I hear they were not that well received.<br /> <br /> Is it possible to make an adventure path for Exalted? I think so, but it ain't gonna be easy!<br /> <br /> You can't tackle an Exalted campaign module like it was DnD. First, the module needs to decide which splat(s) it is meant for. A solar campaign is a lot different from a DB one. I think the module should also have a summary about which Abilities mesh well with the adventure, which might cause problems and which are irrelevant. Your standard linear encounter progression gets too constrained with Exalted characters, especially Celestials. Thus I think GUMSHOE-like scene-chart is a better fit: instead of a linear plot progression, the module should present a problem for the players and a few ways it could be solved by different PC's.<br /> <br /> Personally I like the challenge and have couple of scenarios in my mind. Once I'm done with my current homebrew project, I intend to write a short campaign module about a lost manse and two Exalted circles trying to claim it. Maybe I'll post it here, when it's ready.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24320-taleksi","name":"Taleksi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24320_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-08T22:49:21-07:00","id":1303720,"title":"","content":"Adventures are totally doable for Exalted. I\u2019m not sure Adventure Paths are, or at least not as easily.<br /> <br /> One of the fundamental differences between Exalted and D&amp;D/Pathfinder is that, in D&amp;D everyone gets better at combat as they increase in level and combat is the main method of solving problems and interacting with the world. Thus, it\u2019s possible to roughly predict the capabilities of a party of 4 to 6 adventurers at any given point along a series of adventures.<br /> <br /> In Exalted, it\u2019s possible to make an Essence 5 Solar that can kill an elder god with a spoon but would lose a fist fight with a child and can easily be provoked and manipulated by anyone even slightly charming. Basically, you can\u2019t reasonably expect characters to have any given capabilities at any given point.<br /> <br /> And that\u2019s not even getting into how well Exalted can break the rails of a game. <br /> <br /> Ultimately to do it, you\u2019ll need to have player buy in. Make sure the adventure\u2019s focus is clearly stated and that the players can create characters appropriately. Give them some notes on what sorts of challenges they can expect from future adventures when they\u2019re spending xp, so they don\u2019t go all in on combat right before things switch to political intrigue or something. And keep an eye out for any \u201cchoke points\u201d in your adventure where highly specific events must unfold in specific ways and get rid of them. Similarly, if there are any points where a specific charm or combo will let you ignore a major obstacle, try to revise things.<br /> <br /> One common example of that is the Solar clarm which infallibly detects whether someone is lying. This isn\u2019t actually a problem for mystery stories, since all the suspects should have some secrets that they\u2019re having to lie about and the charm doesn\u2019t tell you what the truth is, but any situation where you\u2019re trying to figure out if a specific person is lying can be short circuited via that charm. <br /> <br /> So maybe skip the plot where the quest giver is lying about everything ...or at least have a backup way into the adventure after they beat/charm the truth out of said quest giver.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-08T22:51:33-07:00","id":1303721,"title":"","content":"I will say that I\u2019m planning on trying my hand at adventure writing once we have the Realm book out. I think DBs will be a bit easier to write for. And I\u2019m gonna see if I can try a set of chained adventures, but who knows how that\u2019ll go.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-09T04:24:45-07:00","id":1303748,"title":"","content":"I don't think the OP is after advice on planning adventures so much as they are looking for existing material. So far in officially published material there is the Tomb of Dreams jumpstart, which is a short adventure suitable for starting characters (it has pre-gen characters but you don't have to use them). It's designed to introduce players to the system. It doesn't lead into a longer story though. <br /> <br /> Other than that, there is community-created content for Exalted on Storyteller's Vault: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?filters=0_45735_0_0_0_0\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.storytellersvault.com/br..._45735_0_0_0_0</a><br /> <br /> I haven't checked it out myself yet, but that would be your most reliable source of ready to play content. <br /> <br /> It's true that Exalted is challenging to write linear stories for, but for the right style of game it's possible, even for Solars (preferably low Essence ones though). &quot;Walking the Earth&quot; type stories where the party is trying to get from A to B to fulfill a goal, and there happens to be an epic road trip in between, or where the party are wandering do-gooders (or ne'er-do-wells) who have to keep moving to stay ahead of the Wyld Hunt, work well for this. You can just say &quot;And this week you come to a village where X is happening&quot; and plop a pre-written module down in front of them. You might have to improvise when they come up with an unorthodox solution, but that's the life of a GM. The main difference in writing that sort of campaign for Exalted is that unless you have a full party of combat monkeys, you need to put just as much emphasis on non-combat. Dungeon crawls totally work in Exalted, but they can't carry a whole campaign.<br /> <br /> The real trouble starts when they settle in one place or with one organisation, and look down at their character sheets and realise they have the tools to break armies and reforge nations. Once you get into that kind of play, you can't rely on pre-written material because player actions will be driving the story, and you'll need to adapt, react, and counter based on the actions of your PCs as they move mountains to fulfill their personal goals, which isn't something an adventure path can do for you. They will change the power dynamics of whatever area they are in by their very nature. Of course, by then you might have your feet under you and be ready to improvise more readily in response to their shenanigans. One good thing about powerful PCs is the plots often write themselves. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}